.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CLIENT\-TYPESCRIPT" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The TypeScript client binding provides a Promise\-based interface to the
bash\-server v2 NDJSON protocol.
It is implemented as the \f[CR]bashclient\f[R] npm package, requires
Node.js 16 or later, and depends only on the Node.js standard library
(\f[CR]net\f[R], \f[CR]child_process\f[R], \f[CR]readline\f[R]).
.PP
The binding uses Promises and async/await throughout.
A background reader loop dispatches incoming messages to per\-channel
Promise queues and registered event callbacks.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Installation
The package is located at \f[CR]bash\-server/clients/typescript/\f[R]
and uses npm:
.IP
.EX
cd bash\-server/clients/typescript
npm install
npm run build
.EE
.PP
This compiles TypeScript sources to both ESM and CommonJS outputs under
\f[CR]dist/\f[R].
The package exports both formats:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqmain\(dq: \(dqdist/cjs/index.js\(dq,
  \(dqmodule\(dq: \(dqdist/esm/index.js\(dq,
  \(dqtypes\(dq: \(dqdist/types/index.d.ts\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
Tests are run with Jest:
.IP
.EX
npm test
.EE
.SH Package structure
.IP
.EX
src/
    index.ts         \-\- Package exports
    client.ts        \-\- BashClient class
    channels/        \-\- Six channel classes
        control.ts
        command.ts
        state.ts
        observe.ts
        debug.ts
        pty.ts
    errors.ts        \-\- Error class hierarchy
    protocol.ts      \-\- NDJSON encode/decode
    transport.ts     \-\- Four transport implementations
    types.ts         \-\- TypeScript interface definitions
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central class is \f[CR]BashClient\f[R].
It owns a \f[CR]Transport\f[R] instance and runs a background reader
loop (a non\-blocking async function) that continuously reads NDJSON
lines from the transport.
.PP
Incoming messages are routed by channel ID:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Request/response messages\f[R] are placed on per\-channel Promise
queues.
Each queue entry is a \f[CR]{ resolve, reject, timer }\f[R] object.
When a channel method sends a request, it creates a new Promise and
pushes it to the queue; the reader loop resolves it when the response
arrives.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Server\-push messages\f[R] (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY
output/exit) are dispatched directly to registered callbacks via the
channel\(cqs \f[CR]dispatch()\f[R] method.
.PP
Each channel is exposed as a public readonly property:
.IP
.EX
client.control   \f[I]// ControlChannel  (CHAN_CONTROL = 0)\f[R]
client.command   \f[I]// CommandChannel  (CHAN_COMMAND = 1)\f[R]
client.state     \f[I]// StateChannel    (CHAN_STATE = 2)\f[R]
client.observe   \f[I]// ObserveChannel  (CHAN_OBSERVE = 3)\f[R]
client.debug     \f[I]// DebugChannel    (CHAN_DEBUG = 4)\f[R]
client.pty       \f[I]// PtyChannel      (CHAN_PTY = 5)\f[R]
.EE
.SH Connection
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] provides four static factory methods for
establishing connections, each returning a
\f[CR]Promise<BashClient>\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Unix domain socket (default)\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);

\f[I]// Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode)\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connectStdio(\(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq);

\f[I]// Inherited file descriptor\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connectFd(3);

\f[I]// Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin)\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connectNamedPipe(\(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq);

\f[I]// From existing transport\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] client = BashClient.fromTransport(myTransport);
.EE
.PP
Note that \f[CR]fromTransport()\f[R] is synchronous \(en it takes an
already\- connected transport and immediately starts the reader loop.
.SH Lifecycle management
TypeScript does not have a built\-in \f[CR]using\f[R] statement
equivalent to Python\(cqs \f[CR]async with\f[R].
Use \f[CR]try\f[R]/\f[CR]finally\f[R] to ensure cleanup:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(socketPath);
\f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token);
    \f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
    console.log(result.stdout);
} \f[B]finally\f[R] {
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.close();
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]close()\f[R] method stops the reader loop, sends a
\f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message, closes the transport, and rejects any
pending Promise queue entries with
\f[CR]TransportError(\(dqconnection closed\(dq)\f[R].
.SH Authentication
After connecting, authenticate before using channels other than
CHAN_CONTROL:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token);
.EE
.PP
The token is a 64\-character hex string.
If authentication fails, \f[CR]AuthError\f[R] is thrown.
Check authentication status with:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (client.isAuthenticated) {
    \f[I]// safe to use all channels\f[R]
}
.EE
