plus arbitrary additional fields.
.SH Error handling
All errors extend \f[CR]BashClientError\f[R], which extends the
built\-in \f[CR]Error\f[R] class:
.IP
.EX
BashClientError
    AuthError          \-\- Authentication failed
    ProtocolError      \-\- Malformed frame or message
    TimeoutError       \-\- Operation timed out
    TransportError     \-\- Socket or I/O error
    ServerError        \-\- Server returned error response
        .channel       \-\- Channel that produced the error
.EE
.PP
Standard \f[CR]try\f[R]/\f[CR]catch\f[R] patterns:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] { AuthError, TimeoutError, ServerError } \f[B]from\f[R] \(dqbashclient\(dq;

\f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(wrongToken);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[B]if\f[R] (e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] AuthError) {
        console.error(\(dqbad token\(dq);
    }
}

\f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(\(dqsleep 60\(dq, 5000);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[B]if\f[R] (e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] TimeoutError) {
        console.error(\(dqcommand timed out\(dq);
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Each error class sets a descriptive \f[CR]name\f[R] property
(\f[CR]\(dqAuthError\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqProtocolError\(dq\f[R], etc.)
for easy identification in stack traces.
